  <div class="teaching-block" id="final-year-projects">
    <h2 class="teaching-block__heading">Final-year projects</h2>
    <div class="page__body">
      <p>Final-year projects in the AIM Lab are drawn from the group's current research. Each project addresses a specific open question, scoped so that a student can complete it within the academic year. Students work with the PhD researcher or postdoc whose research the project extends, use the group's equipment and code, and present their results at group meetings.</p>
      <h3 class="subheading">What a project looks like</h3>
      <p>Most projects combine two of the group's three research areas: geometry processing, dissipative and multistable mechanics, and fabrication. Typical examples are the design, printing, and testing of a flat pattern that deploys into a prescribed curved surface, or a reduced-order model of a multistable lattice validated against experiments in the lab. Purely computational and purely experimental projects are also offered; most projects include both a fabricated specimen and a measurement.</p>
      <h3 class="subheading">Who can apply</h3>
      <p>Final-year students in the Department of Aeronautics at Imperial College London, through the department's project allocation process. Students from other departments may apply; joint supervision can usually be arranged. No specific prior experience is required. The methods used - finite element analysis, additive manufacturing, geometry processing - are taught during the project.</p>
      <h3 class="subheading">Current topics</h3>
      <p>Project topics for the coming year are being finalised. <a href="/contact/">Contact us</a> for the current list, or propose a topic within the group's research areas. UROP placements and summer research are described under <a href="/join-us/undergraduate/">Join us</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="teaching-block" id="masters-projects">
    <h2 class="teaching-block__heading">Master's projects</h2>
    <div class="page__body">
      <p>Master's projects in the AIM Lab are scoped to produce a defined result within a single research term: a validated model, a working prototype, or a measured effect. Projects fall within the group's three research areas - geometry processing, dissipative and multistable mechanics, and fabrication - and are supervised jointly by Tian Chen and the lab member whose research the project extends.</p>
      <h3 class="subheading">How the projects run</h3>
      <p>Each student is assigned a supervising researcher and a defined research question at the start. The first weeks are used to reproduce an existing result with the relevant tools; the remainder of the project extends it. Students have access to the group's fabrication equipment - the multimaterial printer, the robotic arm, and the magnetiser - and to its simulation codes. Reports are written in the format of a short paper.</p>
      <h3 class="subheading">Who can apply</h3>
      <p>Students on the MSc programmes of the Department of Aeronautics at Imperial College London, and MEng students taking a research project. Students on other Imperial programmes may apply; co-supervision is often possible.</p>
      <h3 class="subheading">Current topics</h3>
      <p>Topics for the coming year are being finalised. <a href="/contact/">Contact us</a> for the current list, or propose a project within the group's research areas. Students considering a PhD should also read <a href="/join-us/phd/">Join us</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
